/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491086 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491086 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491086 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491086 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186490740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491086 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d25989c9-ac7b-5cae-ab6b-b2623e213802" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491121 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491121 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85305" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491121 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491121 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the region $D,$ given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)\\le 1,\\,2{e}^{x}\\le y\\le 5{e}^{x}\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere $u=\\frac{y}{{e}^{x}}$ and $v=x-2y.$ Find the representation of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491121 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "171517bd-e706-5408-b35c-f81cec428f88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491121 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that ${T}^{-1}(D)$ is the image of $D$ under the action of the transformation ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nLet's find the image of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane. To do that, we write the region $D$ in terms of $u$ and $v,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(D) & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)\\le 1,\\,2{e}^{x}\\le y\\le 5{e}^{x}\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,{e}^{0}\\le {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)}\\le {e}^{1},\\,\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{{e}^{x}}\\le \\frac{y}{{e}^{x}}\\le \\frac{5{e}^{x}}{{e}^{x}}\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le x-2y\\le e,\\,2\\le \\frac{y}{{e}^{x}}\\le 5\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le v\\le e,\\,2\\le u\\le 5\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 5,\\,1\\le v\\le e\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491130 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491130 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161088" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491130 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491130 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region $D$ enclosed in the first quadrant of the $xy$-plane between the curves\n\n$$\nx=3y,\\,x=7y,\\,x=\\frac{4}{{y}^{2}},\\,x=\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nA transformation $T,$ where\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nmaps a rectangular region $Δ$ with sides parallel to the $u$- and $v$-axes from the $uv$-plane onto $D$ in the $xy$-plane. Given that\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (\\,\\bbox[5pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,,\\,\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{v}{u}}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhat could be the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491130 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ef3f070-3842-5746-bbe4-723d2d023508" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491130 #edb/ref 1074 "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v\\le d\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u(x,y)\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v(x,y)\\le d\\},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\nu(x,y)=a,\\,u(x,y)=b,\\,v(x,y)=c,\\,v(x,y)=d.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D.$ \n\nRewriting the equations \n\n$$\nx=3y,\\,x=7y,\\,x=\\frac{4}{{y}^{2}},\\,x=\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{y}=3,\\,\\frac{x}{y}=7,\\,x{y}^{2}=4,\\,x{y}^{2}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put $u(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ and $v(x,y)=x{y}^{2},$ we can define the transformation ${T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to (\\,\\frac{x}{y}\\,,\\,x{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nTo find $T,$ we should express $x$ and $y$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$ Recall that we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ v=x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, $x=uy.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\nv=u{y}^{3}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{3}=\\frac{v}{u}\\,⟹\\,y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{v}{u}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{v}{u}}$ back into $x=uy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=u\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{v}{u}}=\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}v}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation $T$ can be given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}v},\\,\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{v}{u}}).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491131 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491131 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85306" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491131 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491131 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the region $D,$ given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le {e}^{3y}\\le 8,\\,0\\le {e}^{x/5}\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere $u={e}^{y}$ and $v={e}^{x}.$ Find the representation of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491131 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "308c3b1f-dc69-5e5d-ac0f-cca362b4ad91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491131 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that ${T}^{-1}(D)$ is the image of $D$ under the action of the transformation ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nLet's find the image of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane. To do that, we write the region $D$ in terms of $u$ and $v,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(D) & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le {e}^{3y}\\le 8,\\,0\\le {e}^{x/5}\\le 2\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le {({e}^{y})}^{3}\\le 8,\\,0\\le {({e}^{x})}^{1/5}\\le 2\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt[3]{1}\\le \\sqrt[3]{{({e}^{y})}^{3}}\\le \\sqrt[3]{8},\\,{0}^{5}\\le {({({e}^{x})}^{1/5})}^{5}\\le {2}^{5}\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le {e}^{y}\\le 2,\\,0\\le {e}^{x}\\le 32\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 32\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491144 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491144 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103966" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491144 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491144 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $D$ be the finite region bounded by the curve \n$y-{({y}^{2}+\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=-5$ and the line $y=4.$ Consider the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere $u={y}^{2}+\\frac{x}{2}$ and $v=y.$ Find the representation of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491144 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4efc1305-a064-5e20-8b2c-ca7aa38971ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491144 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{2}+\\frac{x}{2}=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{({y}^{2}+\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=-5\\,⟹\\,v-{u}^{2}=-5\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=4\\,⟹\\,v=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{2}-5 \\\\ v=4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 3 \\\\ v=4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 3,4).$\n\n![](images/fe/fe39efb538d3270028fa5892402d56a8ae0e061968ecca8c676d8ad9f482644e.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-3\\le u\\le 3,\\,{u}^{2}-5\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491164 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491164 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103977" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491164 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491164 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $D$ be the finite region bounded by the curve \n$({x}^{3}+y)^{2}+y=3$ and the line $y=2.$ Consider the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere $u={x}^{3}+y$ and $v=2-y.$ Find the representation of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491164 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "77327bb6-1e9a-5178-984c-098fa761c5e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491164 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+y \\\\ 2-y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${x}^{3}+y=u$ and $y=2-v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{3}+y)^{2}+y=3\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}+2-v=3\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=2-v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2\\,⟹\\,2-v=2\\,⟹\\,v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{2}-1 \\\\ v=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 1 \\\\ v=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,0).$\n\n![](images/60/60d8d3e30eba31683d1b6bb9bf399aa20f2bda37be1cb31bec431a4e27c0f54e.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{2}-1\\le v\\le 0\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491167 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491167 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103916" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491167 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491167 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the region $D,$ given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le {x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\le 4,\\,0\\le {e}^{xy}\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere $u=xy$ and $v={e}^{2xy}.$ Find the representation of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491167 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7c6e5eb4-b450-508c-8a0f-3ca8849b044c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491167 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that ${T}^{-1}(D)$ is the image of $D$ under the action of the transformation ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nLet's find the image of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane. To do that, we write the region $D$ in terms of $u$ and $v,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(D) & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le {x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\le 4,\\,0\\le {e}^{xy}\\le 2\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le (xy)^{2}\\le 4,\\,0\\le {({e}^{xy})}^{2}\\le {2}^{2}\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le {u}^{2}\\le 4,\\,0\\le {e}^{2xy}\\le 4\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $0\\le {x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\le 4,$ we must have $-2\\le xy\\ge 2.$ Then, $-2\\le u\\le 2$ too." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491169 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491169 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161074" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491169 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491169 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region $D$ enclosed in the first quadrant of the $xy$-plane between the lines and curves\n\n$$\n2y=x+1,\\,2y=x+3,\\,y=\\frac{1}{x},\\,y=\\frac{5}{x}.\n$$\n\nA transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nmaps a rectangular region $Δ$ with sides parallel to the $u$- and $v$-axes from the $uv$-plane onto $D$ in the $xy$-plane. Given that\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to (\\,\\bbox[5pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,,\\,xy),\n$$\n\nwhat could be the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491169 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7fe852c9-f9c5-516e-be6d-ac44e221645a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491169 #edb/ref 1074 "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v\\le d\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u(x,y)\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v(x,y)\\le d\\},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\nu(x,y)=a,\\,u(x,y)=b,\\,v(x,y)=c,\\,v(x,y)=d.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D.$ \n\nRe-writing the equations \n\n$$\n2y=x+1,\\,2y=x+3,\\,y=\\frac{1}{x},\\,y=\\frac{5}{x}\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-x=1,\\,2y-x=3,\\,xy=1,\\,xy=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put $u(x,y)=2y-x$ and $v(x,y)=xy,$ we can define the transformation ${T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to (\\,2y-x\\,,\\,xy).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491173 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491173 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83638" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491173 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491173 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $D$ be the finite region bounded by the curve \n$({x}^{3}-y)^{4}-y=1$ and the line $y=2.$ Consider the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere $u={x}^{3}-y$ and $v=y.$ Find the representation of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491173 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8586cda1-86eb-51fb-b15b-c80e0240542a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491173 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${x}^{3}-y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{3}-y)^{4}-y=1\\,⟹\\,{u}^{4}-v=1\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{4}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2\\,⟹\\,v=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{4}-1 \\\\ v=2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm \\sqrt[4]{3} \\\\ v=2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm \\sqrt[4]{3},2).$\n\n![](images/5a/5a2e3fd6f3669f38834359f69e68b01a1e0d1438023418c5234d4071182f4808.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt[4]{3}\\le u\\le \\sqrt[4]{3},\\,{u}^{4}-1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491193 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491193 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103933" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491193 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491193 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the region $D,$ given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2y\\le x\\le 3y,\\,2\\le {4}^{xy}\\le 16\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere $u=\\frac{x}{y}$ and $v=xy.$ Find the representation of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491193 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b81d0c3e-2a7e-5a88-b47d-b7de3ec8925d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491193 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that ${T}^{-1}(D)$ is the image of $D$ under the action of the transformation ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nLet's find the image of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane. To do that, we write the region $D$ in terms of $u$ and $v,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(D) & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2y\\le x\\le 3y,\\,2\\le {4}^{xy}\\le 16\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le \\frac{x}{y}\\le 3,\\,{2}^{1}\\le {2}^{2xy}\\le {2}^{4}\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le 2xy\\le 4\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\le xy\\le 2\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491196 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491196 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83634" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491196 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491196 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the region $D,$ given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,-4\\le y-x\\le -1\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere $u=x+y+1$ and $v=y-x.$ Find the representation of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491196 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c625d289-ee92-5b87-a0d1-89bb85d080fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491196 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that ${T}^{-1}(D)$ is the image of $D$ under the action of the transformation ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nLet's find the image of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane. To do that, we write the region $D$ in terms of $u$ and $v,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(D) & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,-4\\le y-x\\le -1\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le (x+y)+1\\le 4,\\,-4\\le y-x\\le -1\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,-4\\le v\\le -1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491212 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491212 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161082" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491212 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491212 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region $D$ enclosed in the first quadrant of the $xy$-plane between the curves and lines\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=x+2,\\,y=x+7.\n$$\n\nA transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nmaps a rectangular region $Δ$ with sides parallel to the $u$- and $v$-axes from the $uv$-plane onto $D$ in the $xy$-plane. Given that\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to ({x}^{2}y\\,,\\,\\bbox[5pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhat could be the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491212 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f19d953d-521c-5438-8b80-3660f96df807" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491212 #edb/ref 1074 "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v\\le d\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u(x,y)\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v(x,y)\\le d\\},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\nu(x,y)=a,\\,u(x,y)=b,\\,v(x,y)=c,\\,v(x,y)=d.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D.$ \n\nRe-writing the equations \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=x+2,\\,y=x+7\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y=2,\\,{x}^{2}y=7,\\,y-x=2,\\,y-x=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put $u(x,y)={x}^{2}y$ and $v(x,y)=y-x,$ we can define the transformation ${T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to (\\,{x}^{2}y\\,,\\,y-x).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
